## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to reason about multi-word bit manipulations and safe 64→128-bit extension without loops.

## Key Concepts
- 128-bit reversal via two 64-bit reversals and swapping halves
- Parallel-bits reversal (mask/shifts)
- Endianness-insensitive mapping of bit indices

## Code Example
```javascript
function reverse64(x) {
  x = (x >> 1n) & 0x5555555555555555n | (x & 0x5555555555555555n) << 1n;
  x = (x >> 2n) & 0x3333333333333333n | (x & 0x3333333333333333n) << 2n;
  x = (x >> 4n) & 0x0F0F0F0F0F0F0F0Fn | (x & 0x0F0F0F0F0F0F0F0Fn) << 4n;
  x = (x >> 8n) & 0x00FF00FF00FF00FFn | (x & 0x00FF00FF00FF00FFn) << 8n;
  x = (x >> 16n) & 0x0000FFFF0000FFFFn | (x & 0x0000FFFF0000FFFFn) << 16n;
  x = (x >> 32n) | (x << 32n);
  return x;
}
function reverse128(hi, lo) {
  const revLo = reverse64(lo);
  const revHi = reverse64(hi);
  return { hi: revLo, lo: revHi };
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you adapt to streaming 128-bit values?
- How to verify correctness with random tests?

## Why This Is Asked

Tests branchless bit-twiddling and cross-half reasoning on a 128-bit type. Parity is a classic low-level primitive with real-world use in checksums and error-detection.

## Key Concepts

- Branchless parity computation
- XOR folding techniques across 64-bit halves
- 128-bit struct handling without loops

## Code Example

```c
static inline unsigned parity128(uint128 x) {
  uint64_t lo = x.lo;
  lo ^= lo >> 32;
  lo ^= lo >> 16;
  lo ^= lo >> 8;
  lo ^= lo >> 4;
  lo ^= lo >> 2;
  lo ^= lo >> 1;
  uint64_t hi = x.hi;
  hi ^= hi >> 32;
  hi ^= hi >> 16;
  hi ^= hi >> 8;
  hi ^= hi >> 4;
  hi ^= hi >> 2;
  hi ^= hi >> 1;
  return (lo & 1) ^ (hi & 1);
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you adapt this to count parity modulo any odd number (e.g., 3) with similar tricks?
- Could you prove the branchless reduction yields correct parity for all 128-bit inputs?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests 128-bit rotation across word boundary without per-bit loops.

## Key Concepts
- 128-bit value as two 64-bit words; cross-word mixing; edge k<64 vs k>=64

## Code Example
```c
typedef struct { uint64_t hi, lo; } uint128_t;
static inline uint128_t rotl128(uint128_t x, unsigned k){
  k %= 128; uint128_t r;
  if (k < 64){
    r.hi = (x.hi << k) | (x.lo >> (64 - k));
    r.lo = (x.lo << k) | (x.hi >> (64 - k));
  } else { unsigned kk = k - 64;
    r.hi = (x.lo << kk) | (x.hi >> (64 - kk));
    r.lo = (x.hi << kk) | (x.lo >> (64 - kk));
  }
  return r;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you test boundary cases k=0, 63, 64, 127?
- Could you adapt to 256-bit using 4x uint64_t without loops?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests precise understanding of bit-rotation, modulo-64 behavior, and edge cases without branching.

## Key Concepts
- Rotation via (x << s) | (x >> (64 - s)) with s = n & 63
- Safe when s = 0 using masking (64 - s) & 63
- No loops or conditionals; only shifts and ORs

## Code Example
```javascript
// JavaScript BigInt version for 64-bit rotation
function rotl64(x, n) {
  const s = BigInt(n & 63);
  const MASK = 0xFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFn;
  return ((x << s) | (x >> ((64n - s) & 63n))) & MASK;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you adapt for 128-bit types?
- What compiler optimizations help ensure constants are folded efficiently?

## Why This Is Asked

- Checks 128-bit handling and branchless bit counting, a common crypto/DB pattern.
- Evaluates ability to compose primitives without loops.

## Key Concepts

- SWAR (SIMD Within A Register) popcount on 64-bit chunks
- Splitting 128-bit into hi/lo and aggregating results
- Constant-time, no branches

## Code Example

```javascript
unsigned int popcount64(uint64_t x) {
  x = x - ((x >> 1) & 0x5555555555555555ULL);
  x = (x & 0x3333333333333333ULL) + ((x >> 2) & 0x3333333333333333ULL);
  x = (x + (x >> 4)) & 0x0f0f0f0f0f0f0f0fULL;
  return (x * 0x0101010101010101ULL) >> 56;
}

unsigned int popcount128(uint128 x) {
  return popcount64(x.hi) + popcount64(x.lo);
}
```

## Why This Is Asked

Tests ability to craft branchless 128-bit comparison, a common low-level operation in high-performance contexts.

## Key Concepts

- Branchless selection using masks
- 128-bit comparison via hi/lo parts
- Handling -1/0/1 outputs reliably

## Code Example

```c
int compare128(uint128 a, uint128 b) {
  int hi = (int)(a.hi > b.hi) - (int)(a.hi < b.hi);
  int lo = (int)(a.lo > b.lo) - (int)(a.lo < b.lo);
  int t  = (hi != 0);
  int m  = -t;
  return (hi & m) | (lo & ~m);
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you adapt to unsigned 128-bit values?
- How would you extend to return an enum instead of int?

## Why This Is Asked

Assesses branchless bit-twiddling across multiword data and solid understanding of SWAR popcount. 

## Key Concepts

- SWAR (single-instruction, multiple-data) population count
- 64-bit limb handling within a 128-bit composite
- Branchless design using masks and shifts

## Code Example

```javascript
typedef struct { uint64_t hi; uint64_t lo; } uint128;
int popcount128(uint128 x) {
  uint64_t a = x.lo;
  a = a - ((a >> 1) & 0x5555555555555555ULL);
  a = (a & 0x3333333333333333ULL) + ((a >> 2) & 0x3333333333333333ULL);
  a = (a + (a >> 4)) & 0x0F0F0F0F0F0F0F0FULL;
  uint64_t loCount = (a * 0x0101010101010101ULL) >> 56;
  uint64_t b = x.hi;
  b = b - ((b >> 1) & 0x5555555555555555ULL);
  b = (b & 0x3333333333333333ULL) + ((b >> 2) & 0x3333333333333333ULL);
  b = (b + (b >> 4)) & 0x0F0F0F0F0F0F0F0FULL;
  uint64_t hiCount = (b * 0x0101010101010101ULL) >> 56;
  return (int)(loCount + hiCount);
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you optimize for wide vectors (e.g., 256-bit) with minimal branching?
- How would you validate correctness across edge cases like zero and all-ones?

## Why This Is Asked

Explores a new angle: 128-bit popcount via per-half 64-bit parallel counting using a fixed, loopless sequence of masks.

## Key Concepts

- Bit-twiddling for popcount
- Handling 128-bit values by splitting into 64-bit halves
- No branches or loops; purely arithmetic masks

## Code Example

```c
typedef struct { uint64_t hi; uint64_t lo; } uint128;

static inline uint32_t popcount64(uint64_t x) {
  x = x - ((x >> 1) & 0x5555555555555555ULL);
  x = (x & 0x3333333333333333ULL) + ((x >> 2) & 0x3333333333333333ULL);
  x = (x + (x >> 4)) & 0x0F0F0F0F0F0F0F0FULL;
  return (x * 0x0101010101010101ULL) >> 56;
}

uint32_t popcount128(uint128 x) {
  return popcount64(x.lo) + popcount64(x.hi);
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you adapt for 256-bit using the same pattern? 
- What are the trade-offs of inlining vs. a dedicated intrinsic on your compiler/architecture?

## Why This Is Asked

Tests ability to perform cross-128-bit bit counting without loops, using portable bit-twiddling. Demonstrates knowledge of XOR for difference, and multi-word popcount without hardware intrinsics.

## Key Concepts

- XOR-based difference
- SWAR-style popcount on 64-bit words
- Combining counts for 128-bit data

## Code Example

```c
unsigned distance128(uint128 a, uint128 b) {
  uint64_t x = a.hi ^ b.hi;
  uint64_t y = a.lo ^ b.lo;
  // SWAR popcount on x
  x = x - ((x >> 1) & 0x5555555555555555ULL);
  x = (x & 0x3333333333333333ULL) + ((x >> 2) & 0x3333333333333333ULL);
  x = (x + (x >> 4)) & 0x0F0F0F0F0F0F0F0FULL;
  x = x + (x >> 8);
  x = x + (x >> 16);
  x = x + (x >> 32);
  // SWAR popcount on y
  y = y - ((y >> 1) & 0x5555555555555555ULL);
  y = (y & 0x3333333333333333ULL) + ((y >> 2) & 0x3333333333333333ULL);
  y = (y + (y >> 4)) & 0x0F0F0F0F0F0F0F0FULL;
  y = y + (y >> 8);
  y = y + (y >> 16);
  y = y + (y >> 32);
  return (unsigned)(x & 0xFF) + (unsigned)(y & 0xFF);
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you generalize to arbitrary n-word widths while maintaining no-loops constraints?
- Could you optimize using compiler intrinsics if available and fall back gracefully? If so, which intrinsics and how would you keep portability?
